	affiliations = {Sun Yat-Sen University, State Key Laboratory of Biocontrol, Guangzhou, Guangdong, China; Technology Center of Zhanjiang Customs District, Zhanjiang, Guangdong, China; Ltd., Guangzhou, Guangdong, China},
	abstract = {The Pacific white shrimp (Litopenaeus vannamei), an economically significant species in aquaculture, has garnered considerable attention in current research regarding the substitution of fishmeal (FM) in its feed. Functional protein peptides (FPP) comprised primarily of enzymolysis chicken chondropeptide and wall-breaking yeast were utilized in an eight-week experiment to investigate the impact of replacing FM with FPP on growth performance, antioxidant capability, non-specific immunity, and hepatopancreas morphology in L. vannamei. Seven experimental diets, labeled as D0, D10, D20, D30, D40, D50, and D100, were formulated by substituting FM at gradient levels (0 %, 10 %, 20 %, 30 %, 40 %, 50 %, and 100 %) with FPP. Substituting FM with fish protein concentrate at levels ranging from 0 % to 20 % did not result in significant impacts on the growth performance, body composition, and protein retention rate of shrimp (P > 0.05). Analysis of antioxidant enzyme activity and the expression of antioxidant-related genes in the hepatopancreas indicated that low levels of substitution (0–20 %) did not impair the antioxidant capacity of the hepatopancreas, while high levels of substitution led to a significant decrease in this capacity (P < 0.05). Replacement levels not exceeding 50 % were found to have no adverse effects on hepatopancreas morphology. In terms of immune gene expression, the down-regulation of apoptosis gene Caspase3 was observed in all groups following substitution (P < 0.05). Notably, inflammation-related genes exhibited significant down-regulation in the high substitution group (D50 and D100) (P < 0.05). The expression results of associated genes demonstrated a significant up-regulation of Toll, MyD88, Pen3a, and IMD across different FPP replacement groups (P < 0.05). Analysis of intestinal flora revealed that dietary FPP altered the abundance of bacteria. The excessive substitution (more than 20 %) increased the abundance of harmful bacteria such as Vibrio, increasing the risk of disease infection in L. vannamei. Overall, replacing 20 % FM with FPP did not negatively affect growth performance and hepatopancreas health, and could enhance immunity of L. vannamei. © 2025},
