	affiliations = {Shanghai Ocean University, Shanghai, China; Shanghai Ocean University, Shanghai, China; Guangdong Ocean University, Laboratory of Aquatic Animal Nutrition and Feed, Zhanjiang, Guangdong, China},
	abstract = {The present study aimed to evaluate the dietary effects of hydroxyproline (Hyp) + vitamin C (VC) and Eucommia ulmoides (EU) on the growth performance and flesh quality of Pacific white shrimp (Litopenaeus vannamei). Based on a practical diet with 200 g/kg fish meal inclusion (control diet), 14 g/kg coated Hyp (50%) + 2 g/kg L-ascorbyl-2-phosphate (38%) (Hyp + VC) or 20 g/kg EU was added to form three iso-nitrogenous diets. Pacific white shrimp with an initial body weight of 0.70 ± 0.10 g were fed the above three diets for 8 weeks. Compared with the control group, the weight gain of shrimp fed with the Hyp + VC diet and the EU diet was increased by 13.9% and 18.4% (p < 0.05), while the feed conversion ratio was decreased by 0.20 and 0.27 (p < 0.05), respectively. In relation to flesh quality, the addition of Hyp + VC significantly increased the flesh contents of total collagen, heat-insoluble collagen, flavor-free amino acids, flesh hardness, muscle fiber density, and glutathione catalase activity (p < 0.05), as well as significantly reducing the cooking loss and muscle fiber diameter (p < 0.05). The EU group also showed a significantly higher flesh hardness, flavor-free amino acid content, muscle fiber density, and total antioxidant capacity, as well as a lower fiber diameter, than the control group (p < 0.05). In conclusion, the dietary supplementation of 14 g/kg coated Hyp (50%) + 2 g/kg L-ascorbyl-2-phosphate (38%) or 20 g/kg EU improved the growth performance and flesh quality of L. vannamei. © 2025 by the authors.},
