	abstract = {In this study, the pellet characteristics of sinking extruded - feed, pelleted feed and their effects on the growth, feed utilization, hepatopancreas and intestinal histology of Pacific white shrimp ( Litopenaeus vannamei) were compared. First, a basal diet was formulated to contain 20% fishmeal, then the diet was made by compressing or extruding to form two sinking diets, named as pelleted feed and sinking extruded -feed. After the evaluation of pellet characteristics, the two diets were fed to shrimp with initial body weight of ( 7.72 ±0. 01) g for 6 weeks. Compared to pelleted feed, sinking extruded - feed showed higher bulk density, pellet durability index, starch gelatinization degree ( P < 0.05 ) and lower percentage of fines and hardness ( P < 0.05 ) , while no difference was detected in dissolution loss for both feeds ( P > 0 . 05 ) . After 6 weeks of feeding, the shrimp fed with sinking extruded - feed showed significantly lower feed conversion ratio (FCR) ( P < 0.05) and numerically higher weight gain ( P > 0 .05) than that fed with pelleted feed. Both groups presented the similar feed intake, survival, meat yield, whole body composition, hepatopancreas activities of digestive enzymes and glucose metabolism - related enzymes ( P > 0.05 ) . In hepatopancreas and intestinal histology, clear structure was observed without obvious injure for both groups. In conclusion, sinking extruded -feed showed better pellet characteristics and utilization (lower FCR) than pelleted feed, and both feeds had the similar effects on hepatopancreas activities of digestive enzymes, glucose metabolism - related enzymes and hepatopancreas and intestinal histology. © 2023 Editorial Department, Chinese Cereals and Oils Association. All rights reserved.},
