	affiliations = {Shanghai Ocean University, College of Fisheries and Life Science, Shanghai, China; Guangdong Academy of Agricultural Sciences, Institute of Animal Science, Guangzhou, Guangdong, China; The Key Laboratory of Animal Nutrition and Feed Science (South China) of Ministry of Agriculture, Guangzhou, China; Guangdong Key Laboratory of Animal Breeding and Nutrition, Guangzhou, Guangdong, China; Guangdong Key Laboratory of Animal Breeding and Nutrition, Guangzhou, Guangdong, China},
	abstract = {The effects of mulberry leaf flavonoids on intestinal mucosal morphology and intestinal flora of Litopenaeus vannamei were evaluated in this study. A total of 800 Litopenaeus vannamei with an initial body weight of (1.32±0.01) g were divided into 5 groups with 4 replicates in each group and 40 shrimps in each replicate. Four diets were formulated to contain 0, 50, 100, 150 and 300 mg/kg diet of mulberry leaf flavonoids. The trial lasted for 50 days. The results showed as follows: 1) dietary mulberry leaf flavonoids could improve the villus development and increase the muscle layer, thickness of intestine of shrimp. The height of villus in the 100 mg/kg group was the highest and significantly higher than that of other groups (P<0.05) , and the thickness of muscle layer in the 50 mg/kg group was the highest and higher significantly than that of other groups (P<0.05). 2) Dietary mulberry leaf flavonoids could increase the intestinal flora diversity of shrimp. The Shannon index was significantly increased and the Simpson index was significantly decreased in the 50 mg/kg group as compared with 0 mg/kg group (P<0.05). 3) Compared with the no added group, adding mulberry leaf flavonoids into the diet could significantly increase the relative abundances of Vibrio (100 and 150 mg/kg) and Shewanella (50, 100 and 150 mg/kg) (P<0.05) , whereas significantly decrease the relative abundances of Lactococcus (100, 150 and 300 mg/kg) and Rhodococcus (50, 100, 150 and 300 mg/kg) (P<0.05). In conclusion, adding 50 mg/kg of mulberry leaf flavonoids into the diet can promote the intestinal development and increase the intestinal flora diversity of Litopenaeus vannamei. © 2020 Chinese Journal of Animal Science and Veterinary Medicine Co., Ltd.. All rights reserved.},
